35,2,1,talk,Minato,,"I found out that people are spending almost an hour every day just copying things that are already written down. Also, some people are doing a lot of work by hand because positions aren't filled. It seems very tiring.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
36,2,1,talk,Jonathan,,"My findings are concerning. Network coverage at customer plants is highly unstable, and getting device approval is a slow process. Furthermore, our in-house developers are fully committed until the third quarter, and we have undocumented interfaces.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
37,2,1,talk,George,,"My findings confirm my fears. The capital budget is frozen, meaning we cannot afford any large upfront investments. We must rely on the recurring software allowance, but we must be wary of per-head licensing costs.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
38,2,1,talk,May,,"My findings are a bit of a wake-up call! It turns out we're great at starting things but terrible at keeping them. Broad tools requiring everyone's input just die, while narrow, specialized tools actually survive. We're ignoring the patterns!","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
39,2,1,talk,Midori,,"I've noticed some frustrating patterns too. We have repeated delivery errors because of inconsistent addresses, and our clerks are wasting time providing the same certificates over and over. It seems we're losing a lot of energy to these small, avoidable mistakes.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
40,2,2,talk,Minato,,"@Midori, it sounds like we both found the same thing. People are doing the same work over and over. If a proposal can stop this, it would make them much happier and less tired.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
41,2,2,talk,Jonathan,,"@Minato, I agree, but we must be cautious. If a solution to those repetitive tasks relies on stable network access at customer sites or immediate developer support, it will only add to the staff's frustration when it inevitably fails.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""Minato""],""raw_differs"":false}"
42,2,2,talk,George,,"@Jonathan, you are correct. Furthermore, if the solution involves per-head licensing, the cost will balloon as we scale. We must avoid any proposal that requires capital expenditure or creates an unsustainable recurring financial burden.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[""Jonathan""],""raw_differs"":false}"
43,2,2,talk,May,,"@George, you're so focused on the pennies! But look at the bigger picture: if we pick a ""cheap"" tool that requires everyone to use it daily, it'll just be another ghost project. We need something narrow and specialized to actually survive.","{""words"":41,""mentions"":[""George""],""raw_differs"":false}"
44,2,2,talk,Midori,,"I agree with May that a narrow focus is better. If we target those specific, repetitive errors I found—like the address inconsistencies—we can provide real relief without needing a massive, complex system that might fail or cost too much.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[""May""],""raw_differs"":false}"
45,2,3,talk,Minato,,"I agree with Midori. If we can find a small, specialized tool that stops the copying and the mistakes, it would be wonderful. I just hope it doesn't create new work for the people already struggling.","{""words"":36,""mentions"":[""Midori""],""raw_differs"":false}"
46,2,3,talk,Jonathan,,"That is the key point. We must avoid any proposal that assumes our developers are free or that customer plant networks are reliable. A ""small"" tool can still fail if its technical dependencies are ignored.","{""words"":35,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
47,2,3,talk,George,,I agree. We must prioritize a solution that is technically self-sufficient and fits within our recurring software allowance. A fixed-fee implementation would be far more predictable than a licensing model that grows with every new user.,"{""words"":36,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
48,2,3,talk,May,,"Exactly! Let's stop pretending we can launch a company-wide revolution. If the proposal is a ""grand vision"" for everyone, I'm voting no. Give me something niche that actually works for a few people, or it's just a waste of time.","{""words"":40,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
49,2,3,talk,Midori,,"I agree. Let's look for a proposal that solves a specific, real-world pain point. If we can fix those daily frictions without needing a huge budget or a perfect network, we'll actually be helping the people on the floor.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
50,2,4,talk,Minato,,"I hope the candidates we see next are like that. I want to support a proposal that takes away the boring, repetitive work so the staff can breathe a little easier. I'm looking forward to seeing the options.","{""words"":38,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
51,2,4,talk,Jonathan,,"I also hope for a practical outcome. However, I will be scrutinizing the technical requirements closely. We cannot afford to promise a solution that depends on resources or connectivity we simply do not have available right now.","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
52,2,4,talk,George,,"I will be looking for a proposal that avoids capital expenditure and per-head licensing. A fixed-fee, specialized solution is the only way to ensure we don't face a financial surprise during next year's renewals. Let us see the candidates.","{""words"":39,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
53,2,4,talk,May,,"I'm ready! Just remember, if a proposal looks like a ""one-size-fits-all"" miracle, it's probably a trap. I'm looking for something lean, mean, and specialized. Let's see if any of these candidates actually have a chance of surviving!","{""words"":37,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
54,2,4,talk,Midori,,"I agree with everyone. I'll be looking for a proposal that addresses those small, daily losses we've discussed. Let's hope we find something that provides genuine relief to the staff without being overly ambitious.","{""words"":34,""mentions"":[],""raw_differs"":false}"
